      "worked": "<strong>Part A — prove these two ARE isomorphic.</strong><br>G1: V = {1, 2, 3, 4}, E = {12, 23, 34, 41}<br>G2: V = {p, q, r, s}, E = {pq, qs, sr, rp}<br><br><em>Step 1:</em> |V| = 4 for both ✅<br><em>Step 2:</em> |E| = 4 for both ✅<br><em>Step 3:</em> degree sequence — G1: deg(1)=2, deg(2)=2, deg(3)=2, deg(4)=2 → (2,2,2,2). G2: deg(p)=2, deg(q)=2, deg(r)=2, deg(s)=2 → (2,2,2,2) ✅<br><em>Step 4 — build the mapping:</em> f(1)=p, f(2)=q, f(3)=s, f(4)=r<br><em>Step 5 — check every edge:</em><br>12 → pq ✅ &nbsp;&nbsp; 23 → qs ✅ &nbsp;&nbsp; 34 → sr ✅ &nbsp;&nbsp; 41 → rp ✅<br>All 4 edges map onto the 4 edges of G2 → <strong>G1 ≅ G2</strong>.<br><br><strong>Part B — same degree sequence, still NOT isomorphic.</strong><br>H1: V = {1,2,3,4,5,6}, E = {12, 23, 34, 45, 56, 61} (one big 6-cycle)<br>H2: V = {a,b,c,d,e,f}, E = {ab, bc, ca, de, ef, fd} (two triangles)<br>Both: 6 vertices, 6 edges, degree sequence (2,2,2,2,2,2) — all three tests pass!<br>But H1 is <strong>connected</strong> (1 component) and H2 has <strong>2 components</strong>. Also H1's shortest cycle has length 6, H2's has length 3.<br>→ <strong>NOT isomorphic.</strong> This proves the degree sequence alone is never sufficient.",
