      "examAnswer": "Two graphs G1 = (V1, E1) and G2 = (V2, E2) are said to be isomorphic if there exists a bijection f : V1 → V2 such that any two vertices u and v are adjacent in G1 if and only if f(u) and f(v) are adjacent in G2; such a mapping is called an isomorphism and preserves the entire structure of the graph. Isomorphic graphs must necessarily have the same number of vertices, the same number of edges and the same degree sequence, but these conditions are necessary and not sufficient, since a cycle of length six and a pair of disjoint triangles share the degree sequence (2,2,2,2,2,2) without being isomorphic. A homomorphism is a more general mapping f : V1 → V2 which requires only that every edge of G1 is carried to an edge of G2, without being required to be one-to-one or to reflect non-adjacency.",
