      "worked": "<strong>Part A — does this graph have an Euler circuit?</strong><br>V = {P, Q, R, S}, E = {PQ, QR, RS, SP, PR} → |E| = 5<br><br><em>Step 1 — is it connected?</em> From P you reach Q, R, S. <strong>Yes.</strong><br><em>Step 2 — find every degree:</em><br>deg(P) = 3 &nbsp;(PQ, SP, PR) → <strong>odd</strong><br>deg(Q) = 2 &nbsp;(PQ, QR) → even<br>deg(R) = 3 &nbsp;(QR, RS, PR) → <strong>odd</strong><br>deg(S) = 2 &nbsp;(RS, SP) → even<br><em>Handshaking check:</em> 3+2+3+2 = 10 = 2 × 5 ✅<br><br><em>Step 3 — apply the rule:</em><br>Odd-degree vertices = P and R → <strong>exactly two</strong>.<br>→ <strong>No Euler circuit</strong> (that needs every degree even).<br>→ <strong>An Euler path DOES exist</strong>, and it must start at P and end at R (or start at R and end at P).<br><br><em>Step 4 — actually write one:</em> P → Q → R → S → P → R<br>Edges used in order: PQ, QR, RS, SP, PR — all <strong>5</strong> edges, none repeated ✅ Starts at P, ends at R ✅<br><br><strong>Part B — now remove edge PR.</strong><br>E = {PQ, QR, RS, SP} → |E| = 4<br>deg(P) = 2, deg(Q) = 2, deg(R) = 2, deg(S) = 2 — <strong>all even</strong>, and still connected.<br>→ <strong>Euler circuit EXISTS.</strong> One is P → Q → R → S → P, using all 4 edges once and returning home ✅<br><br><strong>Part C — Hamiltonian check on the Part A graph.</strong><br>Try P → Q → R → S. Every vertex visited exactly once → <strong>Hamiltonian path ✅</strong><br>Try P → Q → R → S → P. Edge SP exists, so we return home → <strong>Hamiltonian circuit ✅</strong><br>Note it uses only 4 of the 5 edges — Hamilton does <em>not</em> care about leftover edges, only about vertices.",
