      "examAnswer": "An Euler path in a graph is a path which traverses every edge of the graph exactly once, and an Euler circuit is an Euler path which begins and ends at the same vertex. A connected graph possesses an Euler circuit if and only if every one of its vertices has even degree, and it possesses an Euler path but no Euler circuit if and only if exactly two of its vertices have odd degree, in which case the path must begin at one odd vertex and terminate at the other. A Hamiltonian path is a path which passes through every vertex of the graph exactly once, and a Hamiltonian circuit is such a path which returns to its starting vertex. The essential distinction is that an Eulerian traversal is concerned with covering all edges whereas a Hamiltonian traversal is concerned with covering all vertices; moreover, no simple necessary and sufficient condition is known for the existence of a Hamiltonian circuit, although Dirac's theorem gives the sufficient condition that every vertex of a graph on n ≥ 3 vertices has degree at least n/2.",
