    {
      "title": "Graph Colouring and Chromatic Number",
      "teacher": "Your college has to make an exam timetable. Two papers can share the same time slot only if no student is taking both. Colour every subject with a 'time slot' colour, making sure clashing subjects never get the same colour, and use as few colours as possible. That is graph colouring — a timetable problem in disguise.",
      "explain": "A <strong>proper colouring</strong> of a graph assigns a colour to every vertex so that <strong>no two adjacent vertices share a colour</strong>. The <strong>chromatic number χ(G)</strong> is the <strong>smallest</strong> number of colours for which a proper colouring exists.",
