      "examAnswer": "A proper vertex colouring of a graph G is an assignment of colours to the vertices such that no two adjacent vertices receive the same colour, and the chromatic number χ(G) is defined as the minimum number of colours for which such a colouring exists. A graph has chromatic number one if and only if it has no edges, and chromatic number two if and only if it is bipartite, which is equivalent to containing no cycle of odd length. The complete graph Kn requires exactly n colours since every pair of its vertices is adjacent, and in general χ(G) ≤ Δ + 1 where Δ is the maximum degree of G. By the Four Colour Theorem, every planar graph can be properly coloured using at most four colours. Graph colouring is applied to problems of examination timetabling, frequency assignment and register allocation in compilers.",
